true_positives.matched_candidate:
canManage() for Groups V2 treats VIEW scope as sufficient for MANAGE, enabling privilege escalation

In GroupPermissionsV2.canManage() (no-arg, global check), the implementation is:
```
public boolean canManage() {
    if (root.hasOneAdminRole(AdminRoles.MANAGE_USERS)) return true;
    return hasPermission(null, AdminPermissionsSchema.VIEW, AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE);
}
```
It checks for either VIEW or MANAGE scope on the all-groups resource, instead of only MANAGE (as correctly done in the per-group overload `canManage(GroupModel group)`, which only checks `AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE`). As a result, an admin who was only granted the 'view' scope on the all-groups ('Groups') resource (intended for read-only access) will also satisfy `canManage()`/`requireManage()`. This method is called directly by `GroupsResource.addTopLevelGroup()` via `auth.groups().requireManage()`, so a user with only VIEW permission on groups would be able to create top-level groups - an unintended privilege escalation. The analogous Users V2 implementation (`UserPermissionsV2.canManage(user)`) correctly checks only MANAGE, confirming this is a copy/paste mistake from `canView()`.

false_positives.candidate:
No validation that registered grant shortcuts are non-null and exactly 2 characters long

In `postInit()`, shortcuts obtained from `OAuth2GrantTypeFactory.getShortcut()` are inserted into `grantsByShortcuts`/`grantsToShortcuts` without any validation of length or null-ness:
```
String grantShortcut = gtf.getShortcut();
grantsByShortcuts.put(grantShortcut, grantName);
grantsToShortcuts.put(grantName, grantShortcut);
```
The downstream decoder (`DefaultTokenContextEncoderProvider.getTokenContextFromTokenId`) hard-codes an expectation of exactly 6 total characters (2+2+2) and will throw `IllegalArgumentException` for any other length, while `encodeTokenId()` has no such check and will happily build a malformed id for any shortcut length. A custom/third-party `OAuth2GrantTypeFactory` that returns a shortcut other than exactly 2 characters (which the companion Javadoc for `getShortcut()` even encourages by suggesting "3-letters") will successfully mint tokens with mis-sized ids that cannot be correctly decoded later, and a null shortcut will throw `NullPointerException` from the `ConcurrentHashMap.put` call during `postInit()`, aborting factory initialization.

